<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="syllabus" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Course Syllabus</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>This is the course syllabus for MTH 496: Capstone in Mathematics,
    Fall Term 2026.  The topic of this course is Groups and Geometry.  This course satisfies
    the Tier II Writing Requirement (writing within the discipline) for
    for a Bachelor's Degree from MSU.  As a capstone course, a recurring
    theme will be the interconnectedness of topics from the core courses 
    (MTH 309, 310, 320) required for a degree in mathematics.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- Include chapters -->
  <subsection xml:id="sec-course-info">
    <title>Course Information</title>

    <p>
      This is the syllabus for <term>Groups and Geometry (MTH 496, section 001)</term> for Fall 2026.
      It is a 3 credit course.
      <!-- use a description list (dl) for short items -->
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Instructor</title>

          <p>
            Prof. Robert Bell, C-305 Wells Hall, <url href="mailto:bellro@msu.edu">bellro@msu.edu</url>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Office Hours</title>

          <p>
            TBD (check back for updates)
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Class Meetings</title>

          <p>
            TR 10:20 -- 11:40 a.m. in A-330 WH
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Course Description</title>

          <p>
            This will be a introduction to geometrical transformations,
            e.g., translations, rotations, and reflections, of Euclidean,
            spherical, and hyperbolic geometry.  The group of all such
            isometric transformations can be studied using abstract group
            theory.  In the opposite direction, we will learn how abstract
            groups can be studied using geometric techniques.  Additionally,
            students will be introduced to writing within the discipline of
            mathematics.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Prerequisites</title>

          <p>
            Students should have completed MTH 309, 310, and 320 (or
            equivalent courses).  Familiarity with group theory is
            not assumed.  
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Textbook and course materials</title>

          <p>
            No textbook is required.  Suggested references will
            be provided.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>

